{"id":"chatcmpl-90ac14f1-740f-4d4c-8b17-830d716cd75b","object":"chat.completion","created":1795244573,"model":"deepseek-v4.1-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","reasoning_content":"","content":"```json\n{\n  \"highlights\": \"This task demonstrates a verification-first strategy for spatial medical image questions: consult a domain expert model to form a hypothesis, then independently ground the finding to confirm laterality before answering. The key insight is that image-left corresponds to patient-right on standard frontal radiographs, so laterality must be resolved via explicit localization rather than assumption. Cross-checking expert opinion against grounded coordinates reduces side-confusion errors.\",\n  \"patterns\": [\n    \"For laterality questions, first ask a domain expert model for presence and side, then independently localize the finding to confirm before committing to an answer.\",\n    \"Translate image coordinates to patient anatomy using standard conventions (image-left = patient-right on frontal views) rather than trusting raw visual position.\",\n    \"Decompose binary choice questions into two sub-decisions: presence of the finding, then its spatial attribute (side/location), resolving each with a separate tool.\",\n    \"Use grounded localization as a tie-breaker/validator when an expert model's verbal answer could be ambiguous about spatial orientation.\"\n  ],\n  \"playbooks\": {\n    \"Medical imaging QA\": \"Pair an expert classifier with a grounding/localization tool; use the expert for hypothesis and grounding for spatial confirmation.\",\n    \"Spatial/laterality questions\": \"Always resolve left/right via explicit coordinate mapping to patient anatomy, never by naive image position.\",\n    \"Binary option selection\": \"Convert the choice into a presence check plus an attribute check, then match the attribute to the correct option.\"\n  },\n  \"checklists\": [\n    \"Confirm the finding is actually present before deciding which side it is on.\",\n    \"Verify laterality by mapping localization coordinates to patient anatomy (image-left = patient-right on frontal views).\"\n  ]\n}\n```"},"finish_reason":"stop","logprobs":null}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":461,"total_tokens":823,"completion_tokens":362,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":0,"image_tokens":0,"text_tokens":461},"completion_tokens_details":{"text_tokens":362}}}